{
  "engine": "airflow",
  "config": "stock",
  "workload": "cpu",
  "concurrency": 20,
  "concurrency_mechanism": "CeleryExecutor. Three caps were aligned on 20 so exactly one of them binds at 20 and none binds lower: core.parallelism=20 (deployment-wide), core.max_active_tasks_per_dag=20 (per DAG) and celery.worker_concurrency=20 (prefork pool size on the single airflow-worker container). default_pool keeps its 128 slots, so pool slots never bind. The Celery prefork pool forks one OS process per task instance, so CPU-bound task bodies get real cores.",
  "settings_changed": {
    "core.parallelism": {
      "default": "32",
      "set_to": "20",
      "why": "hard cap on concurrently running task instances across the whole deployment"
    },
    "core.max_active_tasks_per_dag": {
      "default": "16",
      "set_to": "20",
      "why": "per-DAG cap; the default 16 would have bound below 20"
    },
    "celery.worker_concurrency": {
      "default": "16",
      "set_to": "20",
      "why": "Celery prefork pool size on the single worker container; the default 16 would have bound below 20"
    },
    "core.max_map_length": {
      "default": "1024",
      "set_to": "2000",
      "why": "dynamic task mapping refuses to expand past this; 2000 inputs need it raised"
    },
    "core.load_examples": {
      "default": "True",
      "set_to": "true (unchanged)",
      "why": "left on: 'stock' is defined as the official compose, which ships 115 example DAGs loaded and re-parsed"
    },
    "scheduler.scheduler_idle_sleep_time": {
      "default": "1",
      "set_to": "1 (unchanged)",
      "why": "left at the stock value"
    },
    "scheduler.max_tis_per_query": {
      "default": "16",
      "set_to": "16 (unchanged)",
      "why": "left at default; measured enqueue batches averaged 9.0, so this cap was never reached"
    },
    "core.default_pool_task_slot_count": {
      "default": "128",
      "set_to": "128 (unchanged)",
      "why": "default_pool has 128 slots, well above 20, so pool slots never bound"
    },
    "AIRFLOW_UID": {
      "default": "50000",
      "set_to": "1000",
      "why": "the step the official docker-compose guide requires on Linux so the mounted dags/ and logs/ are writable; not a tuning"
    }
  },
  "notes": "Same shape as the sleep workload but tp_cpu, body = naive recursive fibo(31) (~135ms of real CPU). All 2000 task instances created up-front; peak concurrency exactly 20, never exceeded; mean concurrency 6.1 of 20. Example DAGs enabled (117 DAGs). Mean host CPU over the span was 5.1 of 32 cores, so ~20 cores were NOT busy: the ~6 concurrent task processes doing ~135ms of work each account for ~2.0 cores of the 5.1, the rest being the scheduler, dag-processor re-parsing 115 example DAGs, the api-server and Postgres.",
  "measurement": "All reported numbers are derived from the Airflow metadata DB (task_instance), not harness wall-clock. throughput = n_success / (max(end_date) - min(start_date)). latency = end_date - queued_dttm. exec = end_date - start_date. Peak and mean concurrency are a sweep line over the (start_date, end_date) intervals. mean_busy_cores_host comes from an independent 1Hz /proc/stat sampler on the host (32 cores, nothing else running).",
  "fanout": "single DAG run; one @task mapped with .expand() over a literal list of 2000 inputs (dynamic task mapping), giving 2000 independent task instances",
  "theoretical_ceiling_per_s": 148.03,
  "theoretical_ceiling_basis": "20 concurrent / 0.135104 (measured median fibo(31) in the worker image) s of work per task",
